WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.990
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Brett from Indianapolis.

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.910
Brett, you on the air.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.959
Speaker 2: Good to hear from you again.

00:00:08.959 --> 00:00:09.959
Pleasure.

00:00:09.959 --> 00:00:10.959
Yeah.

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:12.570
So I am going to be quick.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:18.230
So I want to ask you, do you think you know
who I refused to say his name.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.650
He just makes me so much.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:26.380
Do you think he will go down as the number
one worst president in history, or do you

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.429
think at least the top five worst?

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:32.050
Speaker 1: Well, the top five worst out of
how many?

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.950
If this country lasts another five thousand
years, it you know, we could have a lot of

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.110
really bad PR.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.079
I just think there's no way to answer those
questions.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:46.480
You know, what we can say is Donald Trump
represented a huge inflection point in the

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.649
basement of the office of the presidency.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.550
He is the worst president in recent political
history.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.230
But any kind of long term pronouncements,
who knows?

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.170
It's very hard to make those predictions and
not particularly useful either.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.840
Speaker 2: Right?

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:09.250
I mean, I feel like, you know you know, a
person like Hitler, he was certainly studied

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.890
a lot.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.610
And there's certainly some changes that were
made after, you know, Germany because of what

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.610
happened to Hitler.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:24.710
And I feel like he could have some repercussions
as to, you know, changes made because of his

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:25.710
presidency.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.710
We will
Speaker 1: see.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.060
We will see there should be consequences both
for Trump.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.290
And I mean, listen, one of the things we learned
under Trump was the safeguards we thought

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.590
we had in place in case like a really horrible
person got elected.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.840
Many of those safeguards clearly aren't strong
enough because Donald Trump so far has skated

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.150
with no consequences.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.390
But we'll see.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:44.390
Hopefully there will be consequences.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.180
Speaker 2: But yeah.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.340
And do you feel that it is important that
we.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:56.360
Give him media attention because I had a German
friend, so I'll sum up the story.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.660
He said, why do you guys still give Trump
attention?

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.500
Speaker 1: Yeah, I've talked about this before.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.610
He is for now the most prominent Republican
and still the leader of the Republican Party,

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.429
which is one of only two major parties in
the U.S.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:16.349
So to just ignore him for the sake of ignoring
him doesn't really make sense, given that

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:22.379
he does have the vast majority of his 74 million
voters still follow the guy and would follow

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:24.480
him to another party or vote for him again.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.870
So he's relevant in American politics.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.439
And I know you can say, well, he's only relevant
because people talk about him.

00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:32.150
He still has seventy four million followers.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.150
That's the reality.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.150
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.099
Of my German friend was like, well, why don't
you guys just ignore him?

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:40.269
And I said, how does it work out for you guys
when you ignored Hitler?

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:42.299
Speaker 1: Yeah, fair.

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:43.299
Fair.

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:44.299
I like that.

00:02:44.299 --> 00:02:45.299
I like where you're going with that, Bret.

00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:46.299
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.299
All right.

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:48.299
They're talking again.

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:49.299
Speaker 1: OK, thanks, Bret.

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:51.630
Bret from Indianapolis getting right to it.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:52.630
Fantastic.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.959
Let's go next to how about Román from Pittsburgh.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:59.599
Roman, you're on the air.

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:02.329
Can you hear me?

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:03.439
Yes, I can.

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:09.420
Speaker 2: So I mostly wanted to talk about
the New York election that's happening.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:15.069
I was very interested in the change in the
voting policies that they are enacting.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:20.799
I think it actually has a lot of potential
nationally, nationally as well, to cause some

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:25.959
actual, like, moderation in the candidates
that kind of have a chance of getting elected.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:29.159
Speaker 1: Sorry, are you talking about the
New York City mayoral race?

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:30.159
Yes.

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:31.790
Oh, and what changes that they make?

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:37.069
I've not been following or maybe I've forgotten
Speaker 2: they transferred to like like a

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.150
standard transferable vote or like
Speaker 1: a single transferable vote, OK?

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.150
Yeah.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.150
Yeah.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.150
Got it.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.150
Yeah, I'm a big advocate of.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:51.439
Speaker 2: Yeah, I was just curious if you
thought that that would have an ability to

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:54.870
affect presidential elections in a positive
way?

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:55.870
Speaker 1: Yes, I do.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.310
And I've been talking about this for a long
time.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:03.540
The combination of doing campaign finance
reform and getting away from first past the

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:09.650
post towards something like single transferable
vote, I think it would it's impossible to

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.659
overstate how much it would do.

00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:16.100
It would allow third parties to really have
a shot.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:20.950
It would make hopefully a lot of Americans
kind of rethink how we approach elections.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:24.690
I've been advocating for for both of those
things for a really long time.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.050
I think it would be great.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:31.240
Speaker 2: Kind of continuing off of that.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.840
Do you think that that that would kind of
hide like so?

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.220
Part of what I think is currently wrong with
the elections is that primaries sort of allow

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:49.770
for the most extreme candidate to push through
a lot of their ideology to because most people

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:58.170
who vote in primaries are either, like highly
engaged in the situation or, you know, have

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.170
more extreme beliefs.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:09.780
Yeah, I think that allowed for Donald Trump
to sort of get, you know, more heinous ideas

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.470
out there to get support from the people who
are more engaged in the and the primaries.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.031
Speaker 1: That may be more true on the Republican
side.

00:05:18.031 --> 00:05:22.400
I mean, look, if you looked at the Democratic
primary of twenty twenty, the guy who had

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.900
more support than we thought was Biden, who
was not the most extreme candidate by any

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:32.390
means, in fact, to a fault, he's completely
uncontroversial and not extreme in any way.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:35.900
So I don't know that that's necessarily true
at the national level.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:40.800
But it's certainly plausible that in certain
maybe within the Republican Party or in certain

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.580
parts of the country, it might be true.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:47.640
Speaker 2: Do you think that might be a result
of the ways that the Democrats have changed

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.730
their primary system?
Speaker 1: Well, tell me how you imagine that

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:52.760
that would be the case.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.270
Speaker 2: One twenty sixteen.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:02.700
There was a very close race between Clinton
and Bernie, and there had been some changes

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.870
that people kind of were insinuating kind
of flattened some of that support so that

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:17.670
it would allow the most popular candidate
to kind of take charge over time.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.850
Speaker 1: Yeah, well, so I don't I don't
know exactly what you're referring to, but

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.930
the superdelegates are voting on the first
round at the convention, which was eliminated

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:26.930
in.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:27.930
Right.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.370
So, yeah, I'm just I'm just not sure about
that.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.890
But all important questions.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.620
Speaker 2: All right.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:41.740
I appreciate hearing from you and you actually
have some or some some support from Pennsylvania

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:46.660
and Pittsburgh, you know, most of that's super
red and super.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:07:32.430
Problematic, but you have some some blue dots
around there.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:55.330
Speaker 1: All right, thank you so much, Román.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:57.199
Appreciate the call.

